counterexample_dsp:
Create one row in each base table so that
  – t4.c1 ('A') is different from t5.c1 ('B');
  – t4.c2 ('B') equals t5.c1, satisfying Q2’s join;
  – t5.c2 = 'Corona' (needed by both queries);
  – NATURAL JOIN fails because c1 values differ (A ≠ B);
  – t6 supplies the same c1 ('A') and a c2 value different from 'Corona' so the NOT IN predicate is true.
All foreign-key and PK constraints are respected.

