counterexample:
/* rows for t1 (owners of the c2 sets) */
INSERT INTO t1 (c1, c2) VALUES
  ('A', 'dummy'),
  ('B', 'dummy');

/* rows for t2 – every c2 that will be referenced from t5 */
INSERT INTO t2 (c1, c2) VALUES
  ('x', 'd'),
  ('y', 'd'),
  ('z', 'd');

/* rows for t5 (define the c2 sets for A and B) */
INSERT INTO t5 (c1, c2, c3) VALUES
  ('A', 'x', 1.00),
  ('A', 'y', 1.00),
  ('B', 'x', 1.00),
  ('B', 'z', 1.00);

counterexample_dsp:
Create two t1 rows A and B.  In t5 give them a partially overlapping c2 set:
  A -> {x , y}
  B -> {x , z}
A and B share c2 = x (so Q2’s join succeeds) and B has an extra value z (so Q2’s HAVING succeeds), but A also has an extra value y that B lacks, therefore A’s c2 set is NOT a subset of B’s.  Q1 rejects the pair (A , B) while Q2 returns it (and also (B , A)).

