explanation:
Q1 looks at every ordered pair (b1 , b2) of rows of t1 and keeps the pair when the set of t5.c2 values attached to b1.c1 is a PROPER subset of the set attached to b2.c1.  Q2 tries to express the same idea, but:
  • it only generates pairs whose first component is lexicographically greater than the second one (alias3.c1 > alias4.c1 in alias2);
  • the final predicate keeps a pair if   barA⊆barB  OR  barB⊄barA (this also keeps the equality case) ;
Taken together, Q2 can miss a legal subset–superset pair whenever the subset-owner happens to be smaller (barA < barB).  Therefore the two queries are not equivalent.

counterexample:
INSERT INTO t1 (c1, c2) VALUES
  ('A', 'dummyA'),
  ('B', 'dummyB');

INSERT INTO t2 (c1, c2) VALUES
  ('X', 'dummyX'),
  ('Y', 'dummyY');

INSERT INTO t5 (c1, c2, c3) VALUES
  ('A', 'X', 1.00),  -- set(A) = {X}
  ('B', 'X', 1.00),  -- set(B) = {X,
  ('B', 'Y', 1.00);  --             Y}

